{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Adjust forecasts downward when sentiment is negative (sentiment < 0.0) or shows early negative trend, as market overreaction often leads to overshoot.",
    "Reduce forecast peak values after sharp spikes (e.g., >70.0) by 5–10% to account for mean reversion or volatility decay.",
    "If true outcome is significantly lower than forecast (e.g., >10% below), apply a downward bias correction, especially if sentiment is neutral or negative.",
    "For long-term horizons (>15 days), reduce forecast volatility by smoothing with 3–5 day moving average of historical prices.",
    "When sentiment is positive (sentiment > 0.0), allow slight upward bias but cap at +10% above recent 7-day average to avoid overestimation.",
    "If forecast consistently overestimates true outcome in early days of the horizon (e.g., day 1–3), apply a 5–10% downward correction to align with observed lag."
  ]
}
